  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_1",
      "name": "dilatancy",
      "category": "deformation-induced volume expansion of particle packing",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "1",
          "section": "Introduction",
          "quote": "The essential feature of dilatancy is that the total volume of the interstices between particles, which are essentially densely packed and individually rigid, expands upon deformation.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "Hall-Petch strengthening",
      "category": "grain-boundary/interface strengthening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "1",
          "section": "Introduction",
          "quote": "the strengthening encountered in polycrystals, the Hall-Petch effect, can also be expected in materials which are not necessarily polycrystalline but, insofar as the effect is concerned, are similar to them",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "Orowan bowing",
      "category": "dislocation-particle interaction",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "2",
          "section": "Analysis",
          "quote": "we can suppose that independent plastic deformation of the matrix can occur by bending dislocations into semicircles of radius L/2",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_4",
      "name": "dislocation climb",
      "category": "diffusional deformation",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "4",
          "section": "Analysis",
          "quote": "First, dislocation climb. The rapid work hardening we have invoked cannot be expected when dislocations climb readily.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_5",
      "name": "diffusional creep",
      "category": "diffusional deformation",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "4",
          "section": "Analysis",
          "quote": "Turning to direct effects of diffusion we remember that diffusional creep rests on the transport of matter between grain boundaries",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_6",
      "name": "work hardening",
      "category": "dislocation accumulation from particle impenetrability",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "as-compacted and frozen",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "3",
          "section": "Analysis",
          "quote": "plastic deformation of the matrix would by and large, induced by circumscribing dislocation, result in an elastic deformation of the particles of like amount. The accompanying reduction in stress in the matrix may be regarded as a work hardening effect.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "fccbf0488c723f06:part:001"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
